First, the system analyzes the incoming query words and compares them against pre-computed statistical term-frequency metadata stored within SQL system tables or metadata catalogs. Next, it applies a probabilistic scoring model, such as a Bayesian inference calculation, to determine the mathematical likelihood that specific database partitions or index ranges contain matching records. The query engine then translates these probability scores into dynamic partition pruning rules, restricting the SQL execution plan to only the highest-probability storage blocks. By targeting these most probable data segments, the database engine avoids costly full-table scans and executes optimized indexed lookups rapidly. Finally, feedback from actual execution results updates the statistical weights, continuously sharpening the routing probabilities for subsequent word searches.
